'''berserker''' (bėr′sėr-kėr), ''n.'' [Also ''berserkir'' and ''berserk,'' < Icel. ''berserkr'' (the E. retaining the nom. suffix ''-r''), pl. ''berserkir;'' commonly explained as ‘bare-sark,’ < ''berr,'' = E. ''bare''<sup>1</sup>, + ''serkr,'' > E. ''sark,'' coat, shirt; but prob. rather ‘bear-sark,’ < *''beri,'' m. (only in comp.) (''bera,'' f.), = AS. ''bera,'' E. ''bear''<sup>2</sup>, + ''serkr.'' “In olden ages athletes and champions used to wear hides of bears, wolves, and reindeer” (Vigfusson). The “berserker’s rage” is expressed by Icel. ''berserksgangr,'' < ''berserkr'' + ''gangr,'' a going, esp. a rapid going, furious rush: see ''gang.''] '''1.''' A wild warrior or champion of heathen times in Scandinavia. {{smaller|In battle the berserkers are said to have been subject to fits of fury, when they howled like wild beasts, foamed at the mouth, gnawed the rim of their shields, etc.; and on such occasions they were popularly believed to be proof against fire and steel. [Commonly written with a capital.]}}
{{smaller block|Out of unhandseled savage nature, out of terrible Druids and ''Berserkirs,'' come at last Alfred and Shakspere.
{{right|''Emerson,'' Misc., p. 85.}}
The wild pirates of the North Sea have become converted into warriors of order and champions of peaceful freedom, exhausting what still remains of the old ''Berserk'' spirit in subduing nature, and turning the wilderness into a garden. {{right|''Huxley,'' Amer. Addresses, p. 124.}}}}
Hence — '''2.''' A person of extreme violence and fury.

'''berstle†''', ''n.'' A variant of ''bristle. Chaucer.''

'''berth'''<sup>1</sup>†, ''n.'' An obsolete spelling of ''birth''<sup>1</sup>.

'''berth'''<sup>2</sup> (bėrth), ''n.'' [First found at the end of

readings.c3s1:
(to keep clear of, keep well away from). (''b'') Room for a vessel to turn around or to ride at anchor. (''c'') A station in which a ship lies or can lie, whether at anchor or at a wharf. (''d'') A room or an apartment in a ship where a number of officers or men mess and reside. (''e'') The shelf-like space allotted to a passenger in a vessel (and hence in a railroad sleeping-car) as a sleeping-place; a sailor’s bunk on board ship; a place for a hammock, or a repository for chests.—'''2.''' A post or an appointment; situation; employment: as, he has got a good ''berth'' at last.—{{smaller|'''Berth and space''', in ''ship-building'', the distance between the molding-edge of one timber and the molding-edge of the one next to it.}}

'''berth'''<sup>2</sup> (bėrth), ''v. t.'' [< ''berth''<sup>2</sup>, ''n.''] ''Naut.'': (''a'') To assign or allot anchoring-ground to; give space to lie in, as a ship in a dock. (''b'') To allot a berth or berths to: as, to ''berth'' a ship’s company.
{{smaller block|The special object of these [changes on the approach of winter] was the economy of fuel and the ''berthing'' of the whole crew below deck.
{{right|''C. F. Hall'', Polar Exp., p. 122.}}}}

